\title{Introduction}
\import{prelude}

\p{A traditional database language (or indeed general-purpose language) has many syntactic classes used for different purposes.
For example, [SQL](sql) has an exceedingly large grammar. A single \code{SELECT} statement has many possible clauses, each with their own syntax (see the [postgresql manual](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries.html)).}

\p{Even principled languages with strong type systems have multiple syntactic classes, depending on whether an operation is happening at the value or the type level. For instance, in Rust one uses \code{Vec<i32>} to apply the \code{Vec : Type -> Type} function to \code{i32}, while ordinary function application looks like \code{plus(1, 2)}. In OCaml one writes the postfix \code{'t list}, while normal function application looks like \code{plus 1 2}.}

\p{Coln has only one syntactic class: the syntactic class of an element of a type. Even types themselves are in this same syntactic class, because they are elements of [type universes](type-universe). What is achieved via syntactic differentiation in other languages is achieved instead by semantic restrictions in Coln.}

\p{\mvrnote{Give a longer example}}
